TY  - BOOK
TI  - Deep learning-driven assistive technology for blind users: Real-time object detection and speech feedback on Android
AU  - Pawar, Diksha
AB  - This study presents the development of a real-time object detection system designed to assist visually impaired individuals using a smartphone-based application. The core objective is to enable users to identify indoor objects and receive audible feedback, thus facilitating safer navigation and greater independence in daily activities. Utilizing the TensorFlow Object Detection API and SSD MobileNet architecture, the proposed system integrates machine learning models optimized through transfer learning to detect and classify 80 different object categories. The application captures live video using the mobile camera, processes it in real-time, and audibly announces the detected objects via text-to-speech technology. A custom dataset comprising over 1,000 annotated images underwent augmentation to enhance model robustness. Evaluation metrics such as precision, recall, F1-score, and mean average precision (mAP) indicate that the system achieves high detection accuracy, with a balanced trade-off between speed and reliability. The Android-based implementation ensures accessibility and cost-effectiveness, eliminating the need for additional hardware or continuous internet access. Furthermore, the system's performance in real-world scenarios demonstrates its practical applicability for the visually impaired. Future enhancements aim to include OCR for reading text, currency recognition, and support for multiple languages. This work contributes to the field of assistive technology by offering a portable, efficient, and scalable solution that leverages mobile computing and deep learning to improve the quality of life for those with visual impairments.
DA  - 2025/05/29/
PY  - 2025
DO  - 10.13140/RG.2.2.28125.12005
DP  - ResearchGate
ST  - Deep Learning-Driven Assistive Technology for Blind Users
ER  -

TY  - CONF
TI  - SSD: Single shot MultiBox detector
AU  - Liu, Wei
AU  - Anguelov, Dragomir
AU  - Erhan, Dumitru
AU  - Szegedy, Christian
AU  - Reed, Scott
AU  - Fu, Cheng-Yang
AU  - Berg, Alexander C.
A2  - Leibe, Bastian
A2  - Matas, Jiri
A2  - Sebe, Nicu
A2  - Welling, Max
AB  - We present a method for detecting objects in images using a single deep neural network. Our approach, named SSD, discretizes the output space of bounding boxes into a set of default boxes over different aspect ratios and scales per feature map location. At prediction time, the network generates scores for the presence of each object category in each default box and produces adjustments to the box to better match the object shape. Additionally, the network combines predictions from multiple feature maps with different resolutions to naturally handle objects of various sizes. SSD is simple relative to methods that require object proposals because it completely eliminates proposal generation and subsequent pixel or feature resampling stages and encapsulates all computation in a single network. This makes SSD easy to train and straightforward to integrate into systems that require a detection component. Experimental results on the PASCAL VOC, COCO, and ILSVRC datasets confirm that SSD has competitive accuracy to methods that utilize an additional object proposal step and is much faster, while providing a unified framework for both training and inference. For $$300 \times 300$$300×300input, SSD achieves 74.3 % mAP on VOC2007 test at 59 FPS on a Nvidia Titan X and for $$512 \times 512$$512×512input, SSD achieves 76.9 % mAP, outperforming a comparable state of the art Faster R-CNN model. Compared to other single stage methods, SSD has much better accuracy even with a smaller input image size. Code is available at https://github.com/weiliu89/caffe/tree/ssd.
